%% Chapter 31: Semantics of Concurrent Languages
\label{ch:concurrent-semantics}

\section{Introduction to Concurrent Semantics}
\label{sec:concurrent-intro}

Sequential semantics---whether operational, denotational, or axiomatic---assumes that computation proceeds in a single, linear sequence of steps. Concurrent languages break this assumption: multiple computations proceed simultaneously, interacting through shared memory, message passing, or synchronization primitives. The semantics of concurrent languages must capture not only the behavior of individual threads but also the ways in which threads interact, interfere, and cooperate.

The study of concurrent semantics is one of the richest and most challenging areas of programming language theory. Unlike sequential computation, where there is essentially one accepted framework (the $\lambda$-calculus or Turing machines), concurrent computation has multiple competing models, each capturing different aspects of concurrency: interleaving, true concurrency, communication, and synchronization.

\begin{historical}
The theory of concurrent computation has multiple origins. Petri nets were introduced by Carl Adam Petri in his 1962 dissertation. Hoare's CSP (Communicating Sequential Processes, 1978) and Milner's CCS (Calculus of Communicating Systems, 1980) provided process-algebraic foundations. The $\pi$-calculus (Milner, Parrow, Walker, 1992) extended CCS with name passing. The actor model (Hewitt, Bishop, Steiger, 1973; Agha, 1986) provided a message-passing foundation. Lamport's work on sequential consistency (1979) and linearizability (Herlihy and Wing, 1990) addressed the semantics of shared-memory concurrency.
\end{historical}

\section{Interleaving Semantics}
\label{sec:interleaving}

\subsection{The Interleaving Model}
\label{sec:interleaving-model}

The simplest approach to concurrent semantics is \emph{interleaving}: the concurrent execution of multiple threads is modeled as all possible sequential interleavings of their individual steps.

\begin{definition}[Interleaving Semantics]
\label{def:interleaving}
Given threads $T_1, \ldots, T_n$ with individual transition relations $\to_1, \ldots, \to_n$, the \textbf{interleaving semantics} defines the concurrent transition relation $\to_C$ as:
\[
\frac{s_1 \to_i s_1'}{ (s_1, \ldots, s_i, \ldots, s_n) \to_C (s_1, \ldots, s_1', \ldots, s_n) }
  \quad \textsc{Interleave}_i
\]
That is, in each step, exactly one thread makes a transition.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Interleaving of Two Threads]
\label{ex:interleaving}
Consider two threads:
\begin{align*}
  T_1 &: x := 1 \\
  T_2 &: x := 2
\end{align*}

The interleaving semantics gives two possible executions:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $T_1$ then $T_2$: final state $x = 2$
  \item $T_2$ then $T_1$: final state $x = 1$
\end{itemize}
The result is nondeterministic: either value is possible.
\end{example}

\subsection{Limitations of Interleaving}
\label{sec:interleaving-limits}

While interleaving is simple and widely used, it has several limitations:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{State space explosion}: The number of interleavings grows exponentially with the number of threads and steps.
  \item \textbf{Loss of concurrency information}: Interleaving cannot distinguish between truly concurrent events and sequential events that happen to be interleaved.
  \item \textbf{Inability to model true concurrency}: Some systems (e.g., hardware with parallel execution units) exhibit behavior that cannot be captured by any interleaving.
\end{itemize}

\section{True Concurrency Models}
\label{sec:true-concurrency}

\subsection{Petri Nets}
\label{sec:petri-nets}

Petri nets provide a graphical and mathematical model of concurrent systems that captures true concurrency---the simultaneous occurrence of independent events.

\begin{definition}[Petri Net]
\label{def:petri-net}
A \textbf{Petri net} is a tuple $(P, T, F, M_0)$ where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $P$ is a finite set of \textbf{places}
  \item $T$ is a finite set of \textbf{transitions} ($P \cap T = \emptyset$)
  \item $F \subseteq (P \times T) \cup (T \times P)$ is a set of \textbf{flow arcs}
  \item $M_0 : P \to \Nat$ is the \textbf{initial marking}
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

A transition $t$ is \emph{enabled} if every input place of $t$ contains at least one token. When $t$ fires, it removes one token from each input place and adds one token to each output place.

\begin{definition}[Concurrency in Petri Nets]
\label{def:petri-concurrency}
Two transitions $t_1$ and $t_2$ are \textbf{concurrent} in marking $M$ if both are enabled in $M$ and they do not share input places. Concurrent transitions can fire simultaneously.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Event Structures}
\label{sec:event-structures}

Event structures, introduced by Nielsen, Plotkin, and Winskel (1981), provide another model of true concurrency.

\begin{definition}[Event Structure]
\label{def:event-structure}
An \textbf{event structure} is a tuple $(E, \leq, \#)$ where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $E$ is a set of \textbf{events}
  \item $\leq$ is a \textbf{causal dependency} relation (a partial order)
  \item $\#$ is a \textbf{conflict} relation (irreflexive and symmetric)
\end{itemize}
satisfying certain consistency conditions.
\end{definition}

Event structures capture three fundamental aspects of concurrency:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Causality}: event $e_1$ must occur before event $e_2$ ($e_1 \leq e_2$)
  \item \textbf{Conflict}: events $e_1$ and $e_2$ are mutually exclusive ($e_1 \mathrel{\#} e_2$)
  \item \textbf{Concurrency}: events that are neither causally related nor in conflict can occur independently
\end{itemize}

\section{Process Algebras}
\label{sec:process-algebras}

Process algebras provide an algebraic framework for describing concurrent systems as compositions of processes.

\subsection{CCS: Calculus of Communicating Systems}
\label{sec:ccs}

CCS, introduced by Milner (1980), is a process algebra based on communication through named channels.

\begin{grammar}{CCS Processes}
  P & ::= \alpha.P \altproduces P_1 + P_2 \altproduces P_1 \mid P_2 \altproduces P \setminus L \altproduces P[f] \altproduces \textbf{0} \altproduces A
\end{grammar}

where $\alpha$ ranges over \emph{actions}: $\alpha ::= a \mid \bar{a} \mid \tau$ (input, output, and internal action).

The key operators are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\alpha.P$: \textbf{prefix}---perform action $\alpha$, then behave as $P$
  \item $P_1 + P_2$: \textbf{choice}---behave as $P_1$ or $P_2$ (environment decides)
  \item $P_1 \mid P_2$: \textbf{parallel composition}---behave as $P_1$ and $P_2$ concurrently, with synchronization on complementary actions
  \item $P \setminus L$: \textbf{restriction}---behave as $P$ but disallow actions in $L$
  \item $P[f]$: \textbf{relabelling}---rename actions according to $f$
\end{itemize}

\begin{semrules}{CCS Operational Semantics}
  \frac{}{\alpha.P \xrightarrow{\alpha} P}
  \quad
  \frac{P \xrightarrow{\alpha} P'}{P + Q \xrightarrow{\alpha} P'}
  \quad
  \frac{P \xrightarrow{\alpha} P'}{P \mid Q \xrightarrow{\alpha} P' \mid Q}
  \quad
  \frac{P \xrightarrow{a} P' \quad Q \xrightarrow{\bar{a}} Q'}{P \mid Q \xrightarrow{\tau} P' \mid Q'}
\end{semrules}

\subsection{CSP: Communicating Sequential Processes}
\label{sec:csp}

CSP, introduced by Hoare (1978), is a process algebra focused on deterministic communication and synchronization.

\begin{grammar}{CSP Processes}
  P & ::= a \to P \altproduces P_1 \sqcap P_2 \altproduces P_1 \sqcup P_2 \altproduces P_1 \parallel_P P_2 \altproduces P_1 ; P_2 \altproduces \textbf{STOP} \altproduces \textbf{SKIP}
\end{grammar}

Key differences from CCS:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Deterministic choice} ($\sqcap$): the process itself decides
  \item \textbf{External choice} ($\sqcup$): the environment decides
  \item \textbf{Interface parallel} ($\parallel_P$): synchronization on shared events
  \item \textbf{Sequential composition} ($;$): $P_1$ followed by $P_2$
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The $\pi$-Calculus}
\label{sec:pi-calculus}

The $\pi$-calculus (Milner, Parrow, Walker, 1992) extends CCS with the ability to pass channel names as values, enabling the dynamic creation of communication topologies.

\begin{grammar}{$\pi$-Calculus Processes}
  P & ::= \bar{x}y.P \altproduces x(y).P \altproduces P_1 \mid P_2 \altproduces \textbf{0} \altproduces \nu x.P \altproduces !P \altproduces [x = y]P
\end{grammar}

Key features:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\bar{x}y.P$: send name $y$ on channel $x$, then continue as $P$
  \item $x(y).P$: receive a name on channel $x$, bind it to $y$, then continue as $P$
  \item $\nu x.P$: create a new private channel $x$ and continue as $P$
  \item $!P$: replicate---behave as infinitely many copies of $P$ in parallel
\end{itemize}

\begin{semrules}{$\pi$-Calculus Communication}
  \frac{}{\bar{x}y.P \mid x(z).Q \longrightarrow P \mid Q[y/z]}
  \quad \textsc{Comm}
\end{semrules}

The $\pi$-calculus is remarkable for its \emph{mobility}: processes can dynamically reconfigure their communication structure by sending and receiving channel names.

\section{Actor Model Semantics}
\label{sec:actor-model}

\subsection{The Actor Model}
\label{sec:actor-model-intro}

The actor model, introduced by Hewitt, Bishop, and Steiger (1973) and developed by Agha (1986), models concurrent computation as a collection of independent \emph{actors} that communicate exclusively through asynchronous message passing.

\begin{definition}[Actor]
\label{def:actor}
An \textbf{actor} is a computational entity that, upon receiving a message, can:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Send a finite number of messages to other actors
  \item Create a finite number of new actors
  \item Designate the behavior to be used for the next message it receives
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Actor Configuration and Transitions}
\label{sec:actor-transitions}

An \emph{actor configuration} consists of a set of actors, each with a mailbox (queue of pending messages) and a behavior (a function from messages to actions).

\begin{definition}[Actor Transition]
\label{def:actor-transition}
An actor transition consists of:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item An actor $A$ receives a message $m$ from its mailbox
  \item $A$'s behavior processes $m$, producing:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item A set of messages to send
      \item A set of new actors to create
      \item A new behavior for $A$
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

The semantics of the actor model is defined by the set of all possible \emph{computations}---sequences of actor transitions. The actor model is inherently nondeterministic: the order in which messages are delivered is not specified.

\subsection{Properties of the Actor Model}
\label{sec:actor-properties}

Key properties of the actor model include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{No shared state}: Actors communicate only through messages, eliminating data races.
  \item \textbf{Asynchronous communication}: Messages are delivered asynchronously; the sender does not wait.
  \item \textbf{Unbounded nondeterminism}: The actor model can express computations with unbounded nondeterminism (e.g., waiting for an external event).
  \item \textbf{Location transparency}: Actors can migrate; message delivery is by actor address, not physical location.
\end{itemize}

\section{Memory Models for Concurrent Programs}
\label{sec:memory-models}

\subsection{Sequential Consistency}
\label{sec:sequential-consistency}

\begin{definition}[Sequential Consistency]
\label{def:seq-consistency}
A multiprocessor system is \textbf{sequentially consistent} if the result of any execution is the same as if the operations of all processors were executed in some sequential order, and the operations of each individual processor appear in the order specified by the program. (Lamport, 1979)
\end{definition}

Sequential consistency is the simplest and most intuitive memory model, but it is too restrictive for modern hardware, which employs various optimizations (store buffers, cache coherence protocols, out-of-order execution) that violate sequential consistency.

\subsection{Relaxed Memory Models}
\label{sec:relaxed-memory}

Modern processors and programming languages use \emph{relaxed memory models} that allow certain reorderings of memory operations:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Total Store Order (TSO)}: Used by SPARC and x86. Allows store-load reordering but preserves all other orderings.
  \item \textbf{Partial Store Order (PSO)}: Allows store-store and store-load reordering.
  \item \textbf{Weak ordering}: Allows all reorderings except those prevented by explicit synchronization.
  \item \textbf{Release consistency}: Synchronizes only at acquire and release points.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The Java Memory Model}
\label{sec:java-memory-model}

The Java Memory Model (JMM), defined by JSR-133 (2004), specifies the semantics of concurrent Java programs. It defines a \emph{happens-before} relation that determines which memory operations are visible to which threads.

\begin{definition}[Happens-Before Relation]
\label{def:happens-before}
The \textbf{happens-before} relation is a partial order on actions, defined as the transitive closure of:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Program order}: Each action in a thread happens-before the next action in the same thread.
  \item \textbf{Synchronization order}: An unlock of a monitor happens-before every subsequent lock of that monitor.
  \item \textbf{Volatile order}: A write to a volatile variable happens-before every subsequent read of that variable.
  \item \textbf{Thread start}: A call to \texttt{Thread.start()} happens-before every action in the started thread.
  \item \textbf{Thread join}: Every action in a thread happens-before the return of \texttt{Thread.join()}.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\section{Linearizability and Sequential Consistency}
\label{sec:linearizability}

\subsection{Linearizability}
\label{sec:linearizability-def}

\begin{definition}[Linearizability]
\label{def:linearizability}
A history $H$ is \textbf{linearizable} if it can be extended (by adding response events) to a history $H'$ that is equivalent to some \textbf{sequential} history $S$ (a history where each invocation immediately precedes its response) such that:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $S$ is a legal sequential specification
  \item $S$ is consistent with the real-time order of $H'$ (if one operation completes before another starts in $H'$, the same order holds in $S$)
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

Linearizability is a \emph{local} property: a concurrent object is linearizable if and only if it can be decomposed into linearizable sub-objects. This makes it compositional and suitable for modular reasoning.

\subsection{Linearizability vs.\ Sequential Consistency}
\label{sec:lin-vs-sc}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{p{3cm}p{5cm}p{5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Property} & \textbf{Sequential Consistency} & \textbf{Linearizability} \\
\hline
Scope & Whole program & Individual objects \\
Compositionality & Not compositional & Compositional \\
Real-time order & Per-thread only & Global \\
Strength & Weaker & Stronger \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Sequential consistency vs.\ linearizability}
\label{tab:lin-vs-sc}
\end{table}

Linearizability implies sequential consistency but not vice versa. The key difference is that linearizability respects the real-time order of operations globally, while sequential consistency only respects per-thread program order.

\subsection{Implementing Linearizable Objects}
\label{sec:linearizable-impl}

Linearizable implementations of concurrent data structures require careful use of synchronization primitives:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Locks}: Provide mutual exclusion; any sequential implementation becomes linearizable when protected by a lock.
  \item \textbf{Compare-and-swap (CAS)}: An atomic operation that reads a memory location, compares it to an expected value, and conditionally writes a new value. CAS enables \emph{lock-free} implementations.
  \item \textbf{Load-linked/store-conditional (LL/SC)}: A pair of operations that provide atomic read-modify-write semantics.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Lock-Free Stack]
\label{ex:lock-free-stack}
A lock-free stack can be implemented using CAS:
\begin{verbatim}
  push(value):
    loop:
      old_top = top
      new_node = Node(value, old_top)
      if CAS(&top, old_top, new_node):
        break
  
  pop():
    loop:
      old_top = top
      if old_top == null: return null
      next = old_top.next
      if CAS(&top, old_top, next):
        return old_top.value
\end{verbatim}
This implementation is linearizable: each push or pop appears to take effect atomically at the point of the successful CAS.
\end{example}

\section{Summary}
\label{sec:concurrent-summary}

The semantics of concurrent languages is a rich and multifaceted field. The key approaches include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Interleaving semantics}: Simple but suffers from state explosion and loss of concurrency information.
  \item \textbf{True concurrency models}: Petri nets and event structures capture the causal structure of concurrent executions.
  \item \textbf{Process algebras}: CCS, CSP, and the $\pi$-calculus provide algebraic frameworks for describing and reasoning about concurrent systems.
  \item \textbf{Actor model}: Provides a message-passing foundation for concurrent and distributed computation.
  \item \textbf{Memory models}: Define the semantics of shared-memory concurrent programs, ranging from sequential consistency to relaxed models.
  \item \textbf{Linearizability}: Provides a compositional correctness criterion for concurrent objects.
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
The theory of concurrent semantics continues to evolve rapidly. Recent developments include: the C11/C++11 memory model (Batty et al., 2011); verified concurrent data structures using separation logic (Jung et al., 2015); the Iris framework for higher-order concurrent separation logic; and the development of causally consistent distributed systems. The interplay between programming language design, hardware architecture, and formal semantics remains one of the most active areas of research in computer science.
\end{historical}

